\title{Disjunctive Sums and the Mirror Principle\\in Well-Founded Impartial Games}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{15 July 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop the outcome theory of disjunctive sums for set-sized, well-founded impartial games, allowing arbitrary branching and game trees of transfinite rank. A position is winning precisely when it has an option that is not winning. We prove that this equation uniquely determines outcomes, disjunctive sums preserve well-foundedness, and the empty game is a two-sided identity for outcomes. Coordinate exchange preserves outcomes in a self-sum. The principal result is a transfinite mirror theorem: every doubled position $(a,a)$ is losing. Thus every opening move on the diagonal gives the opponent a winning position and an explicit mirroring response. For the countdown game on natural numbers, we obtain the exact two-heap law: $(m,n)$ is winning if and only if $m\ne n$. This supplies counterexamples to two plausible composition rules: two winning components can have a losing sum, and an arbitrary losing position need not be neutral. We conclude with algorithms, applications to adversarial planning, and a program toward ordinal Sprague--Grundy theory.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

The classical rule for a terminating impartial game is simple: a position is winning when it has a move to a losing position. Two mathematical tasks lie beneath this rule. One must justify the recursion when the game tree is not finite, and one must understand how outcomes behave when independent games are combined and a player may move in either component.

The appropriate termination hypothesis is well-foundedness. A well-founded game has no infinite legal play, but it may have infinitely many options, arbitrarily long finite plays, and ordinal rank greater than every natural number. Well-founded recursion extends ordinary backward induction to this setting while preserving the operational meaning of normal play.

The disjunctive sum is the fundamental composition operation. A state is a pair, and a turn changes exactly one coordinate. Although termination is preserved and component order does not affect outcomes, the labels ``winning'' and ``losing'' interact with addition in nonmonotone ways. Winning plus winning need not be winning, and losing does not mean neutral.

Our main theorem identifies a universal positive principle. Two identical copies of any well-founded impartial position form a losing sum. The responding player mirrors each move in the untouched copy, restoring equality after every pair of turns. Well-foundedness guarantees that these responses cannot continue forever. No finite bound on the tree height is assumed.

The countdown game gives a complete model. A heap of size $m$ may be replaced by any smaller natural number. For two heaps, equality is exactly the losing condition. If the heaps differ, reduce the larger to the smaller; if they agree, mirror.

\section{Well-founded games and recursive outcomes}

\begin{definition}
A \emph{set-sized impartial game} is a pair $(P,\to)$, where $P$ is a set of positions and $p\to q$ means that $q$ is a legal option from $p$. The same move relation is available to both players. Under normal play, a player with no legal move loses.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
The move relation is \emph{well-founded} if no infinite sequence
\[
p_0\to p_1\to p_2\to\cdots
\]
exists. Equivalently, every nonempty subset $S\subseteq P$ contains a position with no move to another member of $S$.
\end{definition}

Well-foundedness need not give a uniform finite bound. A root can have, for each $n$, an option leading to a path of length $n$. Every play terminates although the root has rank $\omega$. Larger ordinal ranks arise from more elaborate well-founded relations.

Let $W(p)$ assert that $p$ is winning for the mover. The defining equation is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:outcome}
W(p)\iff \exists q\,\bigl(p\to q\ \wedge\ \neg W(q)\bigr).
\end{equation}
Negating it yields
\[
\neg W(p)\iff \forall q\,\bigl(p\to q\Rightarrow W(q)\bigr).
\]
Terminal positions are consequently losing.

\begin{theorem}[Outcome existence]
Every well-founded impartial game has an outcome classification satisfying \eqref{eq:outcome}.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Use well-founded recursion. When evaluating $p$, the values at all legal options are already available because those options are lower in the move relation. Declare $p$ winning exactly when at least one option is losing. This follows the relation itself and therefore applies at every ordinal rank.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness of recursive outcomes]\label{thm:unique}
If $X:P\to\{\mathrm{false},\mathrm{true}\}$ satisfies
\[
X(p)\iff\exists q\,(p\to q\wedge\neg X(q))
\]
for every $p$, then $X(p)\iff W(p)$ for every $p$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If the classifications disagree, choose a move-minimal position $p$ where they disagree. They agree at every option of $p$. Their recursive equations therefore have equivalent right-hand sides at $p$, forcing agreement there as well, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\section{Disjunctive addition}

\begin{definition}
For games $(P,\to_P)$ and $(Q,\to_Q)$, their \emph{disjunctive sum} has positions $P\times Q$. Its moves are
\[
(p,q)\to(p',q)\quad\text{when }p\to_Pp',
\qquad
(p,q)\to(p,q')\quad\text{when }q\to_Qq'.
\]
Thus exactly one coordinate changes per turn.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Preservation of well-foundedness]\label{thm:sumwf}
The disjunctive sum of two well-founded games is well-founded.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Color each move of a hypothetical infinite sum play by the coordinate it changes. One color occurs infinitely often. The corresponding coordinate values form an infinite descending play in that component, contradicting its well-foundedness.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:sumwf} makes the recursive sum outcome $W_{P+Q}(p,q)$ well-defined even for transfinite ranks.

\begin{definition}
The \emph{empty game} $0$ is the one-position game whose sole position $\ast$ has no legal options.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Two-sided terminal identity]\label{thm:identity}
For every $p$ in a well-founded impartial game,
\[
W_{P+0}(p,\ast)\iff W_P(p),
\qquad
W_{0+P}(\ast,p)\iff W_P(p).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Every move from $(p,\ast)$ changes $p$, since the empty coordinate has no options. Hence $(p,\ast)\mapsto W_P(p)$ satisfies the sum's recursive equation. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:unique}. The left identity is symmetric.
\end{proof}

For a self-sum, write $\sigma(p,q)=(q,p)$.

\begin{lemma}[Move symmetry]
A self-sum move $x\to y$ exists if and only if $\sigma(x)\to\sigma(y)$ exists.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A move in the left coordinate becomes a move in the right coordinate after swapping, and conversely.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Outcome commutativity]\label{thm:comm}
For every $a,b\in P$,
\[
W_{P+P}(a,b)\iff W_{P+P}(b,a).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Define $X(a,b)=W_{P+P}(b,a)$. Move symmetry makes $X$ satisfy the same recursive equation as $W_{P+P}$. Uniqueness gives equality.
\end{proof}

\section{The transfinite mirror principle}

\begin{theorem}[Transfinite Mirror Theorem]\label{thm:mirror}
For every position $a$ in every well-founded impartial game, the doubled position is losing:
\[
\neg W_{P+P}(a,a).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Use well-founded induction on $a$. An opening from $(a,a)$ produces $(b,a)$ or $(a,b)$ for some option $a\to b$. The opponent moves in the untouched coordinate to $(b,b)$. By the induction hypothesis, $(b,b)$ is losing. Every option from $(a,a)$ is therefore winning, so $(a,a)$ is losing. The induction follows the original move relation and is independent of finite depth.
\end{proof}

A global contradiction proof gives the same conclusion. If a winning diagonal existed, the recursive equation and a mirror response would produce a smaller winning diagonal. Iterating would create an infinite sequence $a_0\to a_1\to\cdots$, contrary to well-foundedness.

Define the mover and opponent conditions by
\[
M(p)\iff\exists q\,(p\to q\wedge\neg W(q)),
\qquad
O(p)\iff\forall q\,(p\to q\Rightarrow W(q)).
\]

\begin{theorem}[Well-founded determinacy]\label{thm:det}
At every position $p$, exactly one alternative holds: either $W(p)$ and $M(p)$ hold, or $\neg W(p)$ and $O(p)$ hold.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $W(p)$ holds, the outcome equation supplies a losing option. If it fails, no losing option exists, so all options are winning. The alternatives are incompatible.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Diagonal opponent control]
Every opening from $(a,a)$ gives the opponent a winning position. The opponent can force victory by repeating the opening move in the other component.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Combine Theorems~\ref{thm:mirror} and~\ref{thm:det}. The explicit response reaches the smaller losing diagonal in the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:mirror}.
\end{proof}

After each response the coordinates agree. Endless mirroring would yield an infinite play in the original game, so the response strategy eventually makes the final move.

\section{The exact two-heap countdown law}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{countdown game} has positions $\mathbb N$ and move relation
\[
m\to n\iff n<m.
\]
In its two-component sum, a move strictly decreases exactly one coordinate.
\end{definition}

The game is well-founded because natural numbers admit no infinite strictly descending sequence.

\begin{theorem}[Two-Heap Countdown Theorem]\label{thm:countdown}
For all $m,n\in\mathbb N$,
\[
W(m,n)\iff m\ne n.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $m=n$, Theorem~\ref{thm:mirror} makes the pair losing. If $m<n$, reduce the second heap to $m$; if $n<m$, reduce the first heap to $n$. Each unequal position therefore has a move to a losing diagonal position.
\end{proof}

The resulting table begins
\[
\begin{array}{c|ccccc}
 m\backslash n&0&1&2&3&4\\\hline
0&L&W&W&W&W\\
1&W&L&W&W&W\\
2&W&W&L&W&W\\
3&W&W&W&L&W\\
4&W&W&W&W&L
\end{array}
\]
with losses exactly on the diagonal.

The theorem gives a constant-operation optimal policy: compare $m$ and $n$, and when they differ replace the larger by the smaller. For $b$-bit integers, comparison has bit complexity $O(b)$.

A finite dynamic program on $0\le m,n\le N$ may instead process states in increasing order of $m+n$, declaring a state winning exactly when it has a losing option. Naive option enumeration uses $O(N^3)$ time and $O(N^2)$ space. The closed form reduces a single query to $O(1)$ arithmetic operations and generation of the complete table to the unavoidable $O(N^2)$ output time.

\section{Two false composition principles}

\begin{proposition}[Winning plus winning can lose]
A one-heap countdown position $1$ is winning, but $(1,1)$ is losing.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
From $1$, move to $0$. Thus each isolated component is winning. The pair $(1,1)$ lies on the losing diagonal of Theorem~\ref{thm:countdown}.
\end{proof}

Consequently,
\[
W(a)\wedge W(b)\not\Rightarrow W(a+b).
\]
The alternation of turns across the combined system permits two isolated advantages to cancel.

\begin{proposition}[A losing position need not be neutral]
The countdown position $0$ is losing, while $(0,1)$ is winning.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Heap $0$ has no move. Since $0\ne1$, Theorem~\ref{thm:countdown} makes $(0,1)$ winning; explicitly, reduce $1$ to $0$.
\end{proof}

This does not conflict with Theorem~\ref{thm:identity}. The empty \emph{game} is neutral because it contributes no move structure. A losing outcome label alone does not encode enough structure to determine behavior under every sum. Nor does the proposition refute the expected closure of two losing positions under addition; it refutes the stronger claim that a losing component may always be dropped.

\section{Algorithms and applications}

A finite well-founded game represented by a directed acyclic graph can be evaluated by memoized depth-first search. Recursively evaluate options until a losing one is found; return winning if such an option exists and losing otherwise. Every reachable vertex is evaluated once and every edge is inspected at most once, giving $O(|V|+|E|)$ time and $O(|V|)$ storage.

A sum graph can contain up to $|P||Q|$ states. For diagonal self-sums, the mirror theorem eliminates this product search entirely: $(a,a)$ is classified immediately. In countdown, the balancing policy similarly replaces search through all options by one comparison.

These facts matter in adversarial planning. A well-founded rank can certify termination beyond a bounded horizon, while duplicated transition systems admit an invariant-preserving response. When two identical systems begin in equal states, a responder need only recognize and replay the adversary's transition in the paired system.

There is also a caution for value-based modeling. A binary winning/losing label predicts isolated play but is not a compositional representation. Coupling can reverse an apparent advantage, and a losing state need not behave like an absent subsystem. Composition requires a richer latent value.

\section{Toward ordinal Grundy theory}

For finite impartial games, the richer invariant is the Grundy value. The natural transfinite candidate is
\[
g(p)=\operatorname{mex}\{g(q):p\to q\},
\]
where $\operatorname{mex}$ is the least ordinal absent from the set of option values. The expected outcome criterion is
\[
W(p)\iff g(p)\ne0.
\]
The anticipated addition law is
\[
g(p+q)=g(p)\mathbin{\oplus}g(q),
\]
where $\oplus$ denotes ordinal nim-addition. It would explain diagonal cancellation through $\alpha\oplus\alpha=0$, characterize heterogeneous sums, and prove that arbitrary sums of losing positions are losing.

The current results impose necessary tests on that program: the construction must preserve well-founded sums, assign zero to the empty game, respect coordinate exchange, recover self-cancellation, and specialize to equality in two-heap countdown. Finite-support indexed sums should then form a commutative monoid of values independent of ordering and bracketing.

A further direction connects terminating transfinite ranks to open infinite play. Termination can be encoded as an open event in a game of length $\omega$, while ordinal rank bounds the recursion needed to extract a strategy. The direct determinacy theorem above isolates the strategic ingredient needed for such a bridge.

\section{Discussion and conclusion}

A substantial portion of impartial game algebra depends only on well-foundedness. Finite branching and finite height are unnecessary for recursive outcomes, determinacy, closure under binary sum, terminal identity, coordinate symmetry, and diagonal cancellation.

The mirror theorem joins a local symmetry with a global termination argument. Locally, every one-coordinate disturbance can be reproduced in the other copy. Globally, well-foundedness prevents endless responses. Without identical move structures there may be no matching response; without termination an endless mirrored play need not yield a normal-play victory.

The countdown theorem is sharp: the diagonal result settles equality, and one balancing move settles inequality. Its counterexamples show why binary outcome labels cannot support a full algebra of composition. An ordinal-valued theory is therefore not optional ornament but the natural next invariant.

In summary, well-founded impartial games support transfinite backward induction through a unique recursive outcome equation. Disjunctive sums remain well-founded, the empty game is outcome-neutral, and component exchange preserves self-sum outcomes. Every doubled position $(a,a)$ is losing under an explicit mirror strategy. For countdown, $W(m,n)$ holds exactly when $m\ne n$. These results establish a rank-independent cancellation principle and a concrete foundation for ordinal nim arithmetic.

\end{document}

